{
  "summary": "Baseline codegen/headroom review: V8 does inline the tiny FNV helper into the hot parser loop, so hashCode's leaf self-time is not the full dependency cost. However, the CPU profile retains 337 samples at the hash body source position and assigns only 1.323% of samples to hash lines; dominant work is parser name validation, attribute-end search, whitespace/character scanning, duplicate checks, and materialization. This evidence does not justify implementing H1A before an end-to-end gate.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "/tmp/stax-attribute-hash-20260913/builds/R1-diagnostic/index.js",
      "description": "Baseline bundle. parseStart hashes each attribute-name code unit at lines 407-408; hashCode is the Math.imul FNV step at lines 1087-1088; string lookup uses hashString at lines 251 and 1090-1093."
    },
    {
      "path": "/Users/senghyunjo/github/stax-xml/packages/stax-xml/src/core/TokenCursor.ts",
      "description": "Baseline TypeScript source matching the diagnostic bundle's attribute hash representation and lookup flow."
    },
    {
      "path": "/tmp/stax-attribute-hash-20260913/profiles/R1-attribute-headroom.cpuprofile",
      "description": "4.03-second, 26,393-sample profile report: parseStart dominates; hashCode is only 4.4 ms self/0.1% as a leaf, with parser and validation stacks dominant."
    },
    {
      "path": "/tmp/stax-attribute-hash-20260913/headroom-source-ticks.json",
      "description": "Source-position aggregation: hash lines [407:1, 1088:337, 408:1, 1087:10], total 26,378 samples, hashFraction 0.0132307."
    },
    {
      "path": "/tmp/stax-attribute-hash-20260913/R1-opt-code.log",
      "description": "V8 diagnostics and optimized ARM64 code. TurboFan explicitly inlines hashCode into parseStart; emitted code contains XOR/multiply plus an embedded-function identity guard."
    },
    {
      "path": "/tmp/stax-attribute-hash-20260913/R1-profile-summary.json",
      "description": "Per-fixture/access sampled stacks. Attribute-heavy paths are led by parseStart and validation; hashCode appears only as a tiny sampled leaf, with no hashString entry."
    },
    {
      "path": "/tmp/stax-attribute-hash-20260913/headroom-counts.json",
      "description": "Instrumented counts show attribute-heavy input performs 2,885,302 hashes over 2,885,302 scanned code units and 560,537 duplicate candidates; counts are workload facts, not speedup evidence."
    }
  ],
  "architecture": "Attribute-name hashing is producer-side parser work in TokenCursor.parseStart: each UTF-16 code unit updates an FNV-1a accumulator before duplicate detection and storage in a Uint32Array. Name-based public lookup separately hashes the requested string with hashString, then scans stored hashes and confirms spans. In optimized parseStart, TurboFan inlines hashCode, while other parser helpers (notably xmlNameCharWidth and its validation dependencies) are also optimized/inlined. The profile therefore reports hash arithmetic partly under parseStart/source positions rather than as a standalone call; parser validation and lookup/materialization remain separate cost centers.",
  "report": "Observed: `R1-attribute-headroom.cpuprofile` reports 4.03 s wall/on-CPU, 26,393 samples, with parseStart at 69.2% inclusive/56.0% self, findValidatedAttributeEnd at 11.4%, attribute at 6.0% self, and hashCode at 0.1% self. Source attribution reports 337 samples at hashCode's Math.imul line plus 10 at its return line and one each at the two call-site lines, for a 0.0132307 fraction. Observed: R1-opt-code.log contains repeated `Considering ... hashCode`, candidate bytecode size 32, and `Inlining ... hashCode into ... parseStart`; ARM64 output shows `eor`, multiplier materialization, and `mul`, followed by a load/compare of the hashCode function object. The latter is an inlining dependency/identity guard, not an out-of-line call. Observed: dominant source positions include attribute charCodeAt (399, 2,975 ticks), XML-name validation (1060, 1,397), attribute-end indexOf (1020, 1,309), whitespace checks (1030, 1,278), duplicate checking (427, 931), and decode/entity paths. Observed: per-access profile summaries show attribute-heavy type/name/full/event dominated by parseStart (66-83 samples), with validation and materialization/attribute work; hashCode appears at 2-4 samples in those small summaries. No hashString optimization/inlining diagnostic or distinct hashString profile entry was found, so lookup-side hashString behavior is missing evidence. Inference: inlining means standalone hashCode self-time understates its arithmetic and dependency effects, but the retained hash source ticks are direct evidence that the sampled arithmetic itself is about 1.3%, not a hidden 5% cost. Inference: removing producer hashes could improve parser time somewhat, but the profile cannot establish >=5% attribute-heavy E2E or >=3% broader accessor geomean, and operation counts cannot be converted to speedup. Missing: the required R1-vs-candidate E2E timings and ordinary <=1.02 CI upper; absent those gates, reject H1A as too small/speculative before source changes."
}

